  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following is only a redox reaction but not a disproportionation reaction?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$4 \\mathrm{H}_3 \\mathrm{PO}_3 \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{H}_3 \\mathrm{PO}_4+\\mathrm{PH}_3$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$2 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}_2 \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{O}_2$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{P}_4+3 \\mathrm{NaOH}+3 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{NaH}_2 \\mathrm{PO}_2+\\mathrm{PH}_3$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{P}_4+8 \\mathrm{SOCl}_2 \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{PCl}_3+2 \\mathrm{~S}_2 \\mathrm{Cl}_2+4 \\mathrm{SO}_2$",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "The reaction $\\mathrm{P}_4+8 \\mathrm{SOCl}_2 \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{PCl}_3+2 \\mathrm{~S}_2 \\mathrm{Cl}_2$ $+4 \\mathrm{SO}_2$ involves change of oxidation state of P from 0 to +3 and that of $S$ from +4 to +2 .\nThus, it is a redox reaction but not a disproportionation reaction."
  }
